d refunds. So `daysSinceCreation` is 0, which should be fine.\n\nThe \"allows refund on exactly day 30\" test backdates to 30 days ago, so `daysSinceCreation` would be 30. With `>=`, it would throw. It should be `>`.\n\n### Bug 3: `BillingService` - `handleStockReserved` is bound with `.bind(this)` for one handler but not for `handleRefund`\n\nLook at `src/billing/service.ts`, line 24:\n```typescript\nthis.eventBus.on(ORDER_EVENTS.STOCK_RESERVED, this.handleStockReserved.bind(this));\nthis.eventBus.on(ORDER_EVENTS.REFUNDED, this.handleRefund);\n```\n\nThe second handler `handleRefund` is NOT bound. When the event bus calls it, `this` will be undefined (the handler module). But wait... let me check the event bus.\n\nThe event bus stores handlers in a module-level Map. When it calls `handler(data)`, it just invokes the function directly. The handler `handleRefund` is a method on BillingService but it's passed without bind, so `this` won't refer to the BillingService instance.\n\nBut does any test actually test the refund event handler path? Let me check...\n\nLooking at `issueRefund` which emits `ORDER_EVENTS.REFUNDED` — and this is called during `refundOrder`. So when an order is refunded, the handleRefund handler should fire.\n\nThe test \"issues refund and updates payment status\" in billing.test.ts calls `billing.chargePayment(...)` directly then `billing.issueRefund(...)`. But `handleRefund` is registered on the event bus, so when `issueRefund` emits `ORDER_EVENTS.REFUNDED`, it would trigger `handleRefund` — but with wrong `this`, so it would fail to find the payment and set the status.\n\nWait, but does this actually cause a test failure? Let me check the test:\n```typescript\nit('issues refund and updates payment status', async () => {\n    await billing.chargePayment('O1', [...], 0, 0, 'card');\n    const refund = await billing.issueRefund('O1', 50);\n    expect(refund?.status).toBe('refunded');\n});\n```\n\n`issueRefund` does:\n1. Get payment from `this.payments` — works, `this` is correct in the method call\n2. Set `payment.status = 'refunded'`\n3. Emit `ORDER_EVENTS.REFUNDED`\n4. Return `{ ...payment }`\n\nThe returned payment has `status: 'refunded'`. So this test should pass regardless of the `handleRefund` binding bug, because `issueRefund` sets the payment status directly before emitting.\n\nBut then, in `handleRefund`, `this.notificationService.sendRefundNotice(...)` would fail because `this` is wrong. And the test \"sends refund notification\" checks for the notification. Let me look at that test:\n\n```typescript\nit('sends refund notification', async () => {\n    await billing.chargePayment('O1', [...], 0, 0, 'card');\n    await billing.issueRefund('O1', 50);\n    const sent = notifications.getSentNotifications();\n    const refundNotice = sent.find((n) => n.type === 'refund');\n    expect(refundNotice).toBeDefined();\n    expect(refundNotice!.message).toContain('50.00');\n});\n```\n\nSo when `issueRefund` emits `ORDER_EVENTS.REFUNDED`, the `handleRefund` handler is called. If `this` is wrong, `this.payments.get(event.orderId)` could crash (TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined) or return undefined. And `this.notificationService` would also crash or be undefined.\n\nActually, since `handleRefund` is registered without `.bind(this)`, when the event bus calls it as `handler(data)`, `this` would be `undefined` in strict mode or the global object. In the `handleRefund` method:\n```typescript\nprivate async handleRefund(data: unknown): Promise<void> {\n    const event = data as { orderId: string; amount: number };\n    const payment = this.payments.get(event.orderId);  // this.payments is undefined -> crash\n```\n\nThis would throw an error. The `issueRefund` method emits the event and then returns. If the handler crashes... hmm, the emit is awaited:\n```typescript\nawait this.eventBus.emit(ORDER_EVENTS.REFUNDED, {\n    orderId,\n    amount,\n    timestamp: Date.now(),\n});\n```\n\nAnd the event bus handler loop: `await handler(data);` will throw. But the `issueRefund` is `await`ed by the test. So this would throw and the test would fail.\n\nWait, but this is only if no other test catches it first. Let me think about which tests call `issueRefund`:\n- `billing.test.ts`: \"issues refund and updates payment status\" — calls `billing.issueRefund` directly\n- `billing.test.ts`: \"sends refund notification\" — calls `billing.issueRefund` directly\n- `orders.test.ts`: \"refunds a cancelled order within 30 days\" — calls `orderService.refundOrder` which calls `billingService.issueRefund`\n- `orders.test.ts`: \"allows refund on exactly day 30\" — calls `orderService.refundOrder`\n- `orders.test.ts`: \"rejects refund after 30-day window\" — calls `orderService.refundOrder`\n\nFor the orders tests, `refundOrder` calls `issueRefund` which emits `ORDER_EVENTS.REFUNDED`. The `handleRefund` handler on the billing service would be called and would crash.\n\nFor the \"sends refund notification\" test, the `handleRefund` crash would propagate up. The test expects refund notice to be in the notifications list. But if `handleRefund` crashes, the emit fails and `issueRefund` would also fail.\n\nActually wait, let me re-read the event bus. It calls handlers sequentially:\n```typescript\nfor (const handler of handlers) {\n    await handler(data);\n}\n```\n\nIf `handleRefund` throws because `this.payments` is undefined, the error propagates up through `emit` to `issueRefund` to the caller.\n\nSo this is a real bug: `handleRefund` needs to be bound.\n\n### Bug 4: `BillingService.handleStockReserved` - wrong `$` in template replacement\n\nWait, let me look at `sendConfirmation` in notifications/service.ts:\n```typescript\nconst message = template\n    .replace('{orderId}', orderId)\n    .replace('{total}', total.toFixed(2));\n```\n\nAnd the template is: `'Your order {orderId} has been confirmed. Total: ${total}'`\n\nWait! The template uses `{orderId}` and `{total}`, but the template string uses `${total}`. Let me re-check:\n\n```\n'Your order {orderId} has been confirmed. Total: ${total}'\n```\n\nActually no, this is a regular string (not a template literal), so `${total}` is literally `${total}`. And the code does:\n```typescript\n.replace('{total}', total.toFixed(2));\n```\n\nIt's looking for `{total}` but the template has `${total}` — so the replacement will fail, and the message will contain `${total}` literally!\n\nLet me check the test:\n```typescript\nit('sends order confirmation', () => {\n    const record = service.sendConfirmation('O1', 99.99);\n    expect(record.type).toBe('confirmation');\n    expect(record.message).toContain('O1');\n    expect(record.message).toContain('99.99');\n});\n```\n\nThis test checks that the message contains '99.99' — if the replacement doesn't work, the message would contain `${total}` instead. So this test would fail.\n\nWait, but the test output showed 12 failures. Let me think about which tests actually fail. The test output cut off some information. Let me run the tests and see the full output.\n\n### Bug 5: `order.service.ts` - `cancelOrder` emits `ORDER_EVENTS.CANCELLED` but the status is not yet updated\n\nLet me look at `cancelOrder`:\n```typescript\nasync cancelOrder(id: string, reason: string): Promise<Order | undefined> {\n    const order = this.repo.findById(id);\n    if (!order) return undefined;\n    if (order.status !== 'confirmed') return undefined;\n\n    // Emit cancellation event, then update status\n    await this.eventBus.emit(ORDER_EVENTS.CANCELLED, {\n        orderId: id,\n        reason,\n        items: order.items.map((i) => ({\n            productId: i.productId,\n            quantity: i.quantity,\n        })),\n        timestamp: Date.now(),\n    });\n\n    this.repo.update(id, { status: 'cancelled' });\n\n    return this.repo.findById(id);\n}\n```\n\nThe event includes items (used by inventory to release stock) but doesn't include `status: 'cancelled'`.\n\nNow look at `handleOrderCancelled` in `inventory/service.ts`:\n```typescript\nprivate async handleOrderCancelled(data: unknown): Promise<void> {\n    const event = data as OrderCancelledEvent;\n    const eventWithItems = data as OrderCancelledEvent & {\n        items?: Array<{ productId: string; quantity: number }>;\n        status?: string;\n    };\n\n    if (eventWithItems.status !== 'cancelled') return;  // <-- THIS CHECK\n\n    if (eventWithItems.items) {\n        for (const item of eventWithItems.items) {\n            this.repo.release(item.productId, item.quantity);\n        }\n    }\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nThe `handleOrderCancelled` handler checks `if (eventWithItems.status !== 'cancelled') return;`. But looking at the `cancelOrder` method, it emits the event WITHOUT a `status` field! So the event has `{ orderId, reason, items, timestamp }` but no `status`.\n\nThis means `status` is `undefined`, which is not `'cancelled'`, so the handler returns early without releasing stock! This would cause the \"releases stock when order is cancelled\" test to fail.\n\nWait, but the test emits the event directly in `inventory.test.ts`:\n```typescript\nawait bus.emit(ORDER_EVENTS.CANCELLED, {\n    orderId: 'O1',\n    reason: 'changed mind',\n    status: 'cancelled',\n    items: [{ productId: 'SKU-1', quantity: 3 }],\n    timestamp: Date.now(),\n});\n```\n\nThat test DOES include `status: 'cancelled'`. So the inventory test passes for stock release.\n\nBut the order test \"releases stock when order is cancelled\" goes through `orderService.cancelOrder` which doesn't include `status: 'cancelled'` in the event. So the stock release would fail.\n\nSo the fix: add `status: 'cancelled'` to the cancel event emitted by `cancelOrder`, OR remove the `status !== 'cancelled'` check from `handleOrderCancelled`.\n\nActually, looking at this more carefully, that check seems intended to ensure we don't process a non-cancellation event. But the `ORDER_EVENTS.CANCELLED` event should always be a cancellation. So maybe the check is unnecessary or it should be checking for the presence of a reason.\n\nOr more likely, the fix is to add `status: 'cancelled'` to the cancellation event in `cancelOrder()`.\n\n### Bug 6: Global handler registry across EventBus instances\n\nThe `globalHandlers` Map is module-level, shared across all EventBus instances. In tests using `beforeEach`, a new `EventBus` is created each time, but handlers are never cleaned up. So handlers accumulate across tests.\n\nLooking at the tests:\n- `inventory.test.ts` creates a bus, creates an InventoryService, calls `registerHandlers()`. This registers handlers on the global registry.\n- `orders.test.ts` creates a bus, creates an InventoryService, calls `registerHandlers()`. This also registers handlers.\n\nIf both run in the same process, they'd interfere. But vitest runs each file separately typically. However, within a file, `beforeEach` creates a new bus but `registerHandlers()` adds more handlers each time without clearing.\n\nWait, let me check the test output again:\n```\nTest Files  3 failed | 2 passed (5)\nTests  12 failed | 22 passed (34)\n```\n\nOnly 3 test files failed. Let me figure out what's in each file:\n- tests/events.test.ts — 5 tests (all about EventBus)\n- tests/inventory.test.ts — 5 tests\n- tests/orders.test.ts — 8 tests (OrderService) + 4 tests (OrderRepository) = 12 tests\n- tests/billing.test.ts — 4 tests (calculateOrderTotal) + 4 tests (BillingService) = 8 tests\n- tests/notifications.test.ts — 4 tests\n\nTotal: 5 + 5 + 12 + 8 + 4 = 34 tests. But some are failing. Let me run the tests again more carefully.\n\nActually, I need to run the tests and see all the failures. Let me do that.\n\nLet me think about what else might be wrong...\n\n### Bug 7: `calculateOrderTotal` - the final total is not rounded\n\nIn `calculator.ts`:\n```typescript\nconst discountAmount = Math.round(subtotal * (discountPercent / 100) * 100) / 100;\nconst afterDiscount = Math.round((subtotal - discountAmount) * 100) / 100;\nconst taxAmount = Math.round(afterDiscount * taxRate * 100) / 100;\nconst total = afterDiscount + taxAmount;\n```\n\n`total` is not rounded. For the test \"handles multiple items with discount and tax\":\n```typescript\n// subtotal = 3*29.99 + 49.99 = 139.96\n// discount = 139.96 * 0.15 = 20.994\n// after discount = 118.966\n// tax = 118.966 * 0.1 = 11.8966\n// total = 130.8626 -> rounded to 130.86\nexpect(result.total).toBe(130.86);\n```\n\nLet me trace through:\n- subtotal = 139.96\n- discountAmount = Math.round(139.96 * 0.15 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(20.994 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(2099.4) / 100 = 2099 / 100 = 20.99\n- afterDiscount = Math.round((139.96 - 20.99) * 100) / 100 = Math.round(118.97 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(11897) / 100 = 118.97\n- taxAmount = Math.round(118.97 * 0.1 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(11.897 * 100) / 100 = 1189.7... hmm\n\nActually wait, 118.97 * 0.1 = 11.897. Math.round(11.897 * 100) = Math.round(1189.7) = 1190. So 11.90\n- total = 118.97 + 11.90 = 130.87\n\nBut expected is 130.86. So that's wrong.\n\nLet me recalculate more precisely:\n- subtotal = 3 * 29.99 + 49.99 = 89.97 + 49.99 = 139.96\n- discount = 139.96 * 0.15 = 20.994\n- discountAmount (rounded to cents) = Math.round(20.994 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(2099.4) / 100\n\nHmm, 2099.4... Math.round rounds to 2099 (since .4 < .5) → 20.99\n\n- afterDiscount (rounded) = Math.round((139.96 - 20.99) * 100) / 100 = Math.round(118.97 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(11897) / 100\n\nWait, 139.96 - 20.99 = 118.97. Math.round(11897) = 11897 (it's an integer). So 118.97.\n\n- taxAmount = Math.round(118.97 * 0.1 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(11.897 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(1189.7) / 100\n\n11.897 * 100 = 1189.7. Math.round(1189.7) = 1190. Tax = 11.90.\n\n- total = 118.97 + 11.90 = 130.87\n\nBut the expected is 130.86. Let me re-check the comment in the test:\n```\n// subtotal = 3*29.99 + 49.99 = 139.96\n// discount = 139.96 * 0.15 = 20.994\n// after discount = 118.966\n// tax = 118.966 * 0.1 = 11.8966\n// total