1, qty 2, available 2→0, SUCCESS\n\nWait, there are 5 handlers from inventory tests, plus 1 from this test's setup = 6 handlers total. The first test only has 5 handlers from inventory tests since this test's own InventoryService is also created in beforeEach.\n\nActually, let me recount. In inventory.test.ts, the beforeEach runs 5 times:\n- After test 1: 2 handlers (1 created + 1 cancelled)\n- After test 2: 4 handlers\n- After test 3: 6 handlers\n- After test 4: 8 handlers\n- After test 5: 10 handlers\n\nBut also, events.test.ts (5 tests) ran before inventory.test.ts:\n- events.test.ts doesn't register ORDER_EVENTS handlers, just test handlers\n\nAnd billing.test.ts (8 tests) ran before orders.test.ts:\n- billing.test.ts registers ORDER_EVENTS.STOCK_RESERVED and ORDER_EVENTS.REFUNDED handlers\n- These don't interfere with ORDER_EVENTS.CREATED or ORDER_EVENTS.CANCELLED\n\nSo as orders.test.ts starts:\n- ORDER_EVENTS.CREATED: 10 handlers (from inventory tests)\n- ORDER_EVENTS.CANCELLED: 10 handlers (from inventory tests)\n\nTest 1 of orders.test.ts: creates a bus, inventoryService, registers handlers (adds 2 more)\n- createOrder creates order, emits ORDER_EVENTS.CREATED\n- 11 handlers fire for CREATED\n- Each tries to reserve 2 stock\n- Handler 1-5: stock goes 10→8→6→4→2→0\n- After handler 5, stock=0. Handler 6 tries: 0 < 2 → FAIL → rolls back ALL 5 successful reservations → stock back to 10.\n- Handlers 7-11: each has stock=10, reserve 2, stock→8... same pattern\n- So all handlers either succeed or roll back. The last handler (from the current setup) is last to fire.\n\nActually wait, after the rollback, the stock is back to 10, then the next handler reserves 2, stock→8, then the next handler... So effectively, the stock goes through a cycle each time.\n\nBut the key question is: does the test pass? Let me trace:\n\nHandler 1: reserve 2 → works (10→8)\nHandler 2: reserve 2 → works (8→6)\n...\nHandler 5: reserve 2 → works (2→0)\nHandler 6: reserve 2 → fails (0 < 2) →